1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,640
The Personal Protective Equipment, PPE, worn by actors in this video was

2
00:00:04,640 --> 00:00:08,640
selected based on a risk assessment of the chemicals used in the experiment

3
00:00:08,640 --> 00:00:14,000
and conditions simulated in the activity. In scenes where the presenter did not wear PPE

4
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:18,000
while in the lab, all chemicals were removed to eliminate chemical hazards.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:23,680
In this video, we'll go over various methods for dealing with lab emergencies.

6
00:00:24,240 --> 00:00:27,600
For this video, we'll assume you've already completed the first three steps of RAM.

7
00:00:28,240 --> 00:00:31,600
Recognize hazards, assess risks, and minimize risks.

8
00:00:34,960 --> 00:00:39,920
Usually when things go wrong in the lab, either someone knew they were doing something unsafe

9
00:00:39,920 --> 00:00:44,560
and did it anyway, or someone didn't know that they were doing something unsafe.

10
00:00:44,560 --> 00:00:48,080
These are preventable situations if one follows the first three steps of RAM,

11
00:00:48,080 --> 00:00:52,320
but equipment failures or unexpected power outages can occur in any case.

12
00:00:52,320 --> 00:00:54,720
It is prudent to prepare for emergencies.

13
00:00:54,720 --> 00:00:58,800
Labs are required to have emergency equipment available and must be inspected regularly

14
00:00:58,800 --> 00:01:03,840
by your institution. Every time you enter a lab, take note of the location of emergency equipment.

15
00:01:03,840 --> 00:01:06,080
This is part of preparing for emergencies.

16
00:01:06,080 --> 00:01:09,600
Now let's look at four common lab emergencies and how to deal with them.

17
00:01:09,600 --> 00:01:14,080
Broken glass spills on people, spills in the lab, and fires.

18
00:01:14,640 --> 00:01:17,920
If any of these occur, be sure to notify your instructor immediately.

19
00:01:21,300 --> 00:01:24,740
Although broken glass usually doesn't qualify as an emergency,

20
00:01:24,740 --> 00:01:27,780
it is a fairly common mishap in labs that require some attention.

21
00:01:28,420 --> 00:01:31,140
One obvious hazard is that broken glass has sharp edges.

22
00:01:32,180 --> 00:01:33,940
If the broken glass is on the floor,

23
00:01:33,940 --> 00:01:37,380
the first action is to isolate the area so that no one walks through the glass.

24
00:01:39,300 --> 00:01:42,740
Inform your instructor about the broken glass and they will help you deal with it.

25
00:01:42,740 --> 00:01:46,260
Cleaning up broken glass is usually done with a brush and dust pan.

26
00:01:46,260 --> 00:01:49,620
To protect others, broken glass is discarded in a special container

27
00:01:49,620 --> 00:01:52,100
and never in a regular waste paper basket.

28
00:01:52,100 --> 00:01:55,860
Broken glass that is contaminated with a chemical deserves special treatment.

29
00:01:55,860 --> 00:01:58,340
Inform your instructor about any such situation.

30
00:02:01,020 --> 00:02:05,260
What should you do if you're exposed to a hazardous chemical as the result of a spill?

31
00:02:05,260 --> 00:02:07,260
You should always be wearing eye protection,

32
00:02:07,260 --> 00:02:11,980
but if a chemical does get in your eyes, use the eyewash station to flush it out.

33
00:02:11,980 --> 00:02:14,780
Be sure to rinse for at least 15 minutes or longer.

34
00:02:14,780 --> 00:02:16,220
This may seem like a long time,

35
00:02:16,220 --> 00:02:18,300
but it can take a while to flush out the chemicals

36
00:02:18,300 --> 00:02:20,940
that may have absorbed into the tissues of your eye.

37
00:02:20,940 --> 00:02:24,140
Your instructor will assist you in seeking medical attention.

38
00:02:24,140 --> 00:02:26,380
If a liquid spills on your hand or lower arm,

39
00:02:26,380 --> 00:02:35,360
go to a sink and flush the area with lots of water.

40
00:02:35,360 --> 00:02:38,720
If you're wearing a long sleeve shirt, it's best to remove the shirt

41
00:02:38,720 --> 00:02:42,320
or you can cut off the sleeve to remove any chemical that's soaked into the fabric.

42
00:02:42,320 --> 00:02:44,320
If the chemical can't be washed off at the sink,

43
00:02:44,320 --> 00:02:46,960
use a safety shower to flush the area with water.

44
00:02:46,960 --> 00:02:50,320
You may need to remove any clothing that's been soaked with the chemical.

45
00:02:50,320 --> 00:02:54,160
And as I learned firsthand, the shower drops a lot of water all at once.

46
00:02:58,720 --> 00:03:03,040
If you spill a dry chemical in your skin, we're just using baking soda here,

47
00:03:03,040 --> 00:03:06,160
it's best to scrape off most of the chemical before rinsing with water.

48
00:03:09,810 --> 00:03:13,010
A scupula, credit card, or student ID does this well.

49
00:03:13,010 --> 00:03:14,450
Be sure to clean them after use.

50
00:03:17,650 --> 00:03:20,210
If liquid spills on the lab bench or on the floor,

51
00:03:20,210 --> 00:03:24,450
this can create a number of unsafe conditions including slipping on a wet floor.

52
00:03:24,450 --> 00:03:27,970
In general, it's not your responsibility to clean up a spill of a liquid.

53
00:03:27,970 --> 00:03:30,690
Your instructor will take care of this using a spill kit.

54
00:03:30,690 --> 00:03:32,690
However, you can do a few things to help.

55
00:03:32,690 --> 00:03:35,010
You should inform other students in the immediate area

56
00:03:35,010 --> 00:03:37,970
and make sure you or someone else informs your instructor.

57
00:03:37,970 --> 00:03:39,970
For a spill on a lab bench or floor,

58
00:03:39,970 --> 00:03:43,250
you can remove items from the immediate area if it's safe to do so.

59
00:03:43,250 --> 00:03:45,490
If you've been trained, you can isolate the spill

60
00:03:45,490 --> 00:03:48,130
by pouring a ring of sand or absorbent around it.

61
00:03:48,690 --> 00:03:51,730
Your instructor will decide if the lab needs to be evacuated.

62
00:03:54,740 --> 00:03:57,140
Fires in academic labs are not very common,

63
00:03:57,140 --> 00:03:59,300
and when they occur, they tend to be small.

64
00:03:59,860 --> 00:04:02,660
However, they will pose a significant risk.

65
00:04:02,660 --> 00:04:06,980
It's best to take steps to prevent fires as well as be prepared to respond to them.

66
00:04:06,980 --> 00:04:10,420
A good way to understand fire is to look at the fire triangle.

67
00:04:10,420 --> 00:04:12,180
Three things are needed to start a fire.

68
00:04:12,820 --> 00:04:17,540
A substance that can burn, an oxidizing agent, and an ignition source.

69
00:04:18,180 --> 00:04:21,380
Let's look at possible sources for each side of the triangle.

70
00:04:21,380 --> 00:04:23,700
First, let's talk about fuel sources.

71
00:04:23,700 --> 00:04:26,660
Some chemicals can burn and some cannot.

72
00:04:26,660 --> 00:04:30,260
Read the label for every chemical you use to determine if it's flammable.

73
00:04:30,260 --> 00:04:33,940
The GHS pictogram on the bottle will also alert you to this hazard

74
00:04:33,940 --> 00:04:38,420
and note how similar the pictograms are for flammable and oxidizer.

75
00:04:38,420 --> 00:04:40,980
Okay, what about an oxidizing agent?

76
00:04:40,980 --> 00:04:43,540
Flammable compounds are oxidized when they burn,

77
00:04:43,540 --> 00:04:46,740
and the most common oxidizing agent is all around us.

78
00:04:46,740 --> 00:04:48,500
It's the oxygen in the air.

79
00:04:48,500 --> 00:04:51,780
Finally, any ignition source will start the fire.

80
00:04:51,780 --> 00:04:55,940
This is usually a flame from a match or Bunsen burner or an electrical spark.

81
00:04:56,660 --> 00:04:59,220
Small, contained fires can be extinguished by simply

82
00:04:59,220 --> 00:05:01,380
placing a watch glass or large beaker over them.

83
00:05:06,290 --> 00:05:08,290
This deprives a fire of oxygen.

84
00:05:08,290 --> 00:05:11,570
Removing any side of the fire triangle extinguishes the fire.

85
00:05:11,650 --> 00:05:14,770
Larger fires will likely require the use of a fire extinguisher.

86
00:05:15,810 --> 00:05:17,890
There are various types of fire extinguishers,

87
00:05:17,890 --> 00:05:22,450
but labs are generally equipped with the A, B, C type.

88
00:05:23,250 --> 00:05:26,290
This will work on everything except active metal fires.

89
00:05:26,290 --> 00:05:30,130
Fire code requires that an appropriate extinguisher be located in all labs.

90
00:05:30,770 --> 00:05:33,490
When using any extinguisher, use the pass technique.

91
00:05:34,290 --> 00:05:37,410
Pull a pin, aim at the base of the fire,

92
00:05:37,410 --> 00:05:42,210
squeeze the handle, and sweep along the base of the fire.

93
00:05:44,500 --> 00:05:45,860
If there's a fire in your lab,

94
00:05:45,860 --> 00:05:48,980
your instructor will probably be the one to use the extinguisher.

95
00:05:48,980 --> 00:05:51,700
You should know the pass technique in case you encounter a fire.

96
00:05:52,420 --> 00:05:56,180
As with other emergencies, your instructor will tell you what to do in case of a fire

97
00:05:56,180 --> 00:05:58,980
and if you are expected to use a fire extinguisher.

98
00:05:58,980 --> 00:06:03,620
On rare occasions, something can happen that requires evacuation of the lab.

99
00:06:03,620 --> 00:06:08,180
If it is safe to do so, turn off your electrical equipment and gas burner,

100
00:06:08,180 --> 00:06:11,300
close the sash when working in a hood, and exit the lab.

101
00:06:13,060 --> 00:06:14,980
As you progress in your science education,

102
00:06:14,980 --> 00:06:18,020
you will learn more about various kinds of lab hazards.

103
00:06:18,020 --> 00:06:20,420
Preparing for emergencies requires forethought.

104
00:06:20,980 --> 00:06:24,740
Responding effectively and safely requires training and practice.

105
00:06:24,740 --> 00:06:27,380
Many scientists can tell stories about lab mishaps.

106
00:06:28,020 --> 00:06:31,460
Following the ramp steps will minimize harm to people and property.

107
00:06:31,460 --> 00:06:33,540
To learn more about the other steps in ramp,

108
00:06:33,540 --> 00:06:36,500
be sure to check out the other five videos in this series.

109
00:06:36,500 --> 00:06:38,420
You can find them by clicking here,

110
00:06:38,420 --> 00:06:43,220
checking out the links in the description, or visiting acs.org slash safety videos.
